<header><p class="st-kicker">city state · Greece and the Aegean</p><h1 class="st-title">Andros</h1><p class="st-meta">900 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.) · capital Andros</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Northernmost large island of the Cyclades, coloniser of the Chalcidice and remembered for defying Themistocles's demand for money in 480 BCE.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Andros, just south-east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euboea">Euboea</a>, was settled by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ionia">Ionian Greeks</a> in the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/iron-age">Iron Age</a>. The fortified Geometric town of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zagora-andros">Zagora</a> flourished in the 8th century BCE. Andros was long linked with <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/eretria">Eretria</a>, and c. 655 BCE Andrians founded colonies in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chalcidice">Chalcidice</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/acanthus-greece">Acanthus</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stagira">Stagira</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/argilus">Argilus</a>.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>The island lived from farming, wine and seafaring. It sent ships to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xerxes-i">Xerxes</a> in 480 BCE; when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/themistocles">Themistocles</a> demanded money, the Andrians replied that their gods were Poverty and Helplessness. Andros later paid tribute in the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/delian-league">Delian League</a>, and <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/empire/athens">Athens</a> planted a colony there c. 450 BCE. The marble <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hermes-of-andros">Hermes of Andros</a> shows its later prosperity.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Under Hellenistic kings Andros changed hands often. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rome">Rome</a> captured it in 199 BCE and handed it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/attalus-i">Attalus I</a> of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/kingdom-of-pergamon">Kingdom of Pergamon</a>, and it passed to direct Roman control after 133 BCE.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><div class="st-emblem" role="img" aria-label="city state, 900 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)"><svg viewBox="0 0 100 100" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M24 82 V42 H32 V34 H40 V42 H46 V34 H54 V42 H60 V34 H68 V42 H76 V82 Z"/><path d="M44 82 V66 Q50 58 56 66 V82"/><path d="M34 54 H40 M60 54 H66"/></svg><p class="st-emblem-type">city state</p><p class="st-emblem-region">Greece and the Aegean</p><div class="st-span" aria-hidden="true"><span class="st-span-bar" style="left:41.79%;width:13.93%"></span></div><div class="st-span-axis" aria-hidden="true"><span>3000 BCE</span><span>1 CE</span><span>2000</span></div></div><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>900 BCE – 200 BCE (approx.)</dd><dt>Centuries</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/10th-century-bce">10th century BCE</a> – <a href="/centuries/3rd-century-bce">3rd century BCE</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>city state</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>mercantile</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Andros</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Greek religion; cult of Dionysus</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>Ionic Greek</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Classical Andros</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=andros" aria-label="Andros on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5302 817 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Andros lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5622 1073L5591 1068M5622 1073L5632 1088M5622 1073L5673 1031"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5591 1068h0M5632 1088h0M5673 1031h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5622 1073h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5622 1073h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Andros. <a href="/atlas?year=-550&amp;focus=andros">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>

